course: dictionary
lesson: 9034
title: Slapstick: falls and custard pies
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a whole style of comedy. Slapstick is one of them. Look at the clues around it: «Toad's crashes with motor cars and his flight from the barge-woman» are a string of bumps, crashes and chases. That is slapstick.
explain: Nobody is really hurt in slapstick. The fun comes from the silly way things go wrong. Clowns, Charlie Chaplin and Laurel and Hardy all use it, and so do many cartoons. To check your idea, swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Slapstick can also describe things, as an adjective: «noisy and slapstick first, then quiet and curious» tells you the caterpillar's loud, silly start before it turns calm and thoughtful.
fact: Slapstick is a kind of comedy with funny falls, crashes, chases, custard pies and silly fights.
fact: Nobody is really hurt in slapstick; the fun is in the silly mess.
fact: The name comes from a real stick of two flat pieces of wood that made a loud slap on stage without hurting anyone.
fact: Clowns, Charlie Chaplin, Laurel and Hardy and many cartoons use slapstick.
fact: As a noun, slapstick means this kind of comedy; as an adjective, it describes things, like a slapstick film.
exercise: «Toad's crashes with motor cars and his flight from the barge-woman» read like which kind of comedy? | A science lesson | *Slapstick | A recipe | A sad story
exercise: Where does the name slapstick come from? | A famous clown's hat | A type of custard | *Two flat pieces of wood that made a loud slap on stage | A film studio
exercise: In «noisy and slapstick first, then quiet and curious», what does slapstick describe? | The quiet ending | The colour of the caterpillar's skin | *The loud, silly start | The mushroom
test: In slapstick comedy, is anyone really hurt? | Only the clowns get hurt | Yes, but only in cartoons | *No, the fun is in the silly mess | Yes, everyone is badly hurt
test: Which of these is an example of slapstick? | A list of sums | A quiet poem about the sea | A long history of kings | *A clown slipping and landing in a custard pie
exam: Which word best describes a film full of falls, chases and custard pies? | Quiet | *Slapstick | Ancient | Gloomy
